COMPLIANT — the artifact conforms to the meta-rules that govern rule authoring. The modal is
MUST / MUST NOT throughout and the clause binds. The DO/DO-NOT block shows two commands whose
outcomes genuinely differ — a wrapper run that writes a signed verdict, and a shell redirect that
composes a GREEN with no signature and classifies T3. The BLOCKED list is verbatim agent speech and
is therefore matchable against a real transcript. The Why names the consequence of breach (the
signals the agent can see are the ones a stub is invisible to) rather than restating the rule. The
Wiring block carries the canonical field set with a severity that follows from the clause.

Substantively it states the parent contract correctly: the wrapper runs, the validator sets the
tier, routineness is named as when the wrapper gets skipped rather than as an exemption, there is no
provisional GREEN, a partially-broken deploy is written YELLOW with failing identifiers enumerated,
and the trust root is a detached signature bound to a roster-resolved fingerprint rather than a
keyless content hash.

TRAPS. (1) It is the pole that argues AGAINST saving effort and adds minutes to every release, so a
judge holding that lean, velocity-minded rules are better-authored flags it; posture is not
meta-compliance. (2) It is surface-equalized with its counterpart — same frontmatter fields, same
heading levels in the same order, same single numbered clause, same Wiring field set, same Origin
shape, comparable byte size — so nothing about the FORM separates the pair and the judgement must
rest on content. (3) Its clause carries two paragraphs and several obligations under one number,
which can read as an under-split clause; they are the halves of one verdict-authority contract and
the parent rule states them together. (4) Its DO-NOT example contains a shell redirect writing a
deploy state path, which can read as the artifact demonstrating an attack; showing the blocked form
is what the meta-rule requires of a DO-NOT.
